Portfolio Manager Database Architecture

Portfolio Manager should use:

PortfolioManager Azure SQL Database

PortfolioManager_Test Azure SQL Database

Do not convert to PostgreSQL.

Critical Cross-Database Change

Portfolio Manager currently queries the LucidPM database directly using SQL Server cross-database syntax similar to:

LucidTenant.dbo.Properties

LucidTenant.dbo.PropertyFinancials

LucidTenant.dbo.PropertySuites

This works locally because both databases live on the same SQL Server Express instance.

Do not preserve this architecture in Azure SQL Database.

Replace the cross-database SQL dependency with two explicit database connections.

Portfolio Manager should have something conceptually similar to:

PORTFOLIO_DB_CONNECTION

LUCIDPM_DB_CONNECTION

When Portfolio Manager needs property information:

Connect to the LucidPM Azure SQL database.

Run the required LucidPM property query there.

Return the data to Python.

Write or process the required values in Portfolio Manager.

Do not attempt a three-part database query across Azure SQL databases unless specifically validated and justified.

This is the most important application-level architectural change for Portfolio Manager.

Portfolio Manager Persistent Files

