Test and Production Separation

Preserve test and production behavior.

LucidPM currently has a database toggle between TenantCRM and TenantCRM_Test.

Do not break this behavior during initial migration.

Portfolio Manager should similarly maintain clear test and production configuration.

Production deployments should default safely to production configuration.

Development and test environments should never accidentally write to production because of a missing environment variable.

Cost Constraints

This project has a strong cost constraint.

Target Azure infrastructure cost:

$0 if possible.

Near $0 if unavoidable.

Use:

Azure SQL free database offer.

Azure Container Apps scale-to-zero.

Azure managed TLS certificates.

Existing domain.

Minimal Blob Storage.

Avoid:

Azure SQL Managed Instance.

Always-on virtual machines.

Premium Container Apps configuration.

Paid Container Registry unless necessary.

Azure Files unless actually required.

Unnecessary private networking during the initial deployment.

Over-engineered enterprise architecture.

Deployment Strategy

Do not move both apps to Azure simultaneously.

Recommended sequence:

Phase 1

Create Azure resources.

Create the shared resource group.

Create Azure SQL logical server.

Create Azure SQL databases.

Do not change local production apps yet.

Phase 2

Migrate a copy of LucidPM TenantCRM into Azure SQL.

Connect to it using SSMS.

Validate schema, data, constraints, identity columns, and queries.

Phase 3

Point a separate local LucidPM configuration at Azure SQL.

Run LucidPM locally against the Azure database.

Validate major application workflows.

Do not replace the local production database yet.

Phase 4

Containerize LucidPM.

Deploy it to Azure Container Apps.

Test using the temporary azurecontainerapps.io hostname.

Do not change the custom domain yet.

Phase 5

Implement Blob Storage for LucidPM documents.

Verify lease source PDFs and generated lease PDFs survive container restarts.

Phase 6

Add authentication if required.

Add LucidPM custom subdomain.

Enable Azure managed HTTPS certificate.

Phase 7

Migrate Portfolio Manager database to Azure SQL.

Validate it independently.

Phase 8

Replace Portfolio Manager cross-database SQL queries with a second explicit connection to the LucidPM Azure SQL database.

Test the property synchronization workflow.

Phase 9

Containerize Portfolio Manager.

Deploy it as a second Azure Container App.

Test through the Azure temporary hostname.

Phase 10

Secure Portfolio Manager.

Add its custom subdomain.

Enable Azure managed HTTPS certificate.

Phase 11

Only after both cloud applications are proven stable should any local production dependency be retired.

