Addendum: Architect Analysis and POC Plan (2026-08-07)

This section was added after reviewing this handoff against the actual LucidPM_Reflex repository. It corrects assumptions in the original document and lays out a scoped proof-of-concept sequence to run before committing to the full phased migration above.

Gap Analysis Findings (LucidPM, grounded in current code)

The Prod/Test database toggle is not environment-variable driven. It is a runtime UI toggle in application state: AppState.use_test_db in state.py, defaulting to Test, with server and database names hardcoded (localhost\SQLEXPRESS, TenantCRM, TenantCRM_Test in state.py lines 11-13). No os.getenv usage exists anywhere in the codebase. The original handoff's assumption of an env-var-driven split does not match reality. This needs a real design decision: cloud builds should select the connection at deploy time via environment variable, with the in-app toggle removed or gated for cloud builds, not just repointed underneath.

The current database connection uses Trusted_Connection=yes (Windows integrated authentication), built in state.py get_conn() (lines 26-35). Azure SQL Database does not support Windows integrated authentication. This is a hard blocker not identified in the original handoff. The connection logic needs a SQL-authentication (or Entra ID authentication) branch added before any Azure SQL connectivity can be tested, not just a server-name swap.

Lease document storage root is itself stored as admin-configurable state in SQL (AppSettings row LeaseDocumentStorageRoot, seeded in pages/lease_documents.py lines 883-886, editable via pages/admin_settings.py), defaulting to a hardcoded local path in pages/lease_documents_pdf.py line 72. Moving to Blob Storage is therefore not just a code change but a data migration of every stored StoredFilePath/RelativePath row, plus a decision about what happens to the existing admin screen that currently lets someone repoint production storage to an arbitrary local path.

The /api/pick-files endpoint in LucidPM_Reflex.py (lines 39-76) shells out to PowerShell to open a native Windows OpenFileDialog, hardcoded to a local Generated folder path. This has no Linux equivalent and no fallback. It will break outright in a Linux container and must be replaced with a browser-native file input before containerization, not discovered after.

The Fernet encryption key used to protect SMTP and AI/API-key configuration is generated and stored in the same AppSettings SQL table as the ciphertext it protects (pages/settings.py lines 35-57, key name LocalEncryptionKey). Key and ciphertext living in the same database defeats the purpose of encrypting at rest. The key should move to Container Apps secrets or Key Vault independent of Azure timing.

No rxconfig.py, run.bat, requirements.txt, or pyproject.toml exists anywhere in the tracked repository. Local Python is 3.14.4 per .venv/pyvenv.cfg, but nothing is actually installed in that venv, so the dependency set can only be reconstructed from source imports (reflex, pyodbc, fastapi, reportlab, pypdf, cryptography). This scaffolding must be created before any Dockerfile can be written.

The tracked repository contains hundreds of versioned duplicate files (_1, _2, _v3_0_18 suffixes) alongside the live unsuffixed modules. Any automated inventory pass (the "Codex Tasks" section above) should be scoped to the live files first or it will drown real findings in noise from dead copies.

No references to Portfolio Manager or cross-database LucidTenant.dbo. queries exist in this repository. That integration lives entirely in the separate Portfolio Manager codebase and was not available to review here.

Additional Deployment Gap: Container Registry Default

az containerapp up, the simplest CLI path to deploy a Container App, provisions an Azure Container Registry by default. ACR is not free, and the original handoff explicitly says to avoid provisioning it automatically. The POC and later phases should build and push images to a free registry instead (GitHub Container Registry or Docker Hub free tier) and point Container Apps at that registry explicitly, rather than using the default az containerapp up flow unmodified.

Recommended Phase 0 (before any Azure resource is touched)

Add requirements.txt or pyproject.toml and rxconfig.py to the repository.

Replace the native PowerShell file-picker endpoint with a browser file input.

Add a SQL-authentication branch to the database connection logic and make the connection target (server, database, auth mode) environment-variable driven, with the existing runtime Prod/Test toggle reviewed and gated for cloud builds.

Move the Fernet encryption key out of the AppSettings table and into secret storage.

None of these require any Azure resource to exist and should happen regardless of migration timing.

Scoped POC Plan (LucidPM only, Portfolio Manager excluded entirely)

The goal of the POC is to de-risk the two unknowns that would sink the full migration if they failed, before investing further: whether the app actually works against Azure SQL, and whether it can be containerized and deployed within the free tier without a paid registry.

POC-A: Create the Azure subscription (user action, portal, requires payment/identity details). Install Azure CLI locally. Run az login. Create the shared resource group Lucido-Apps-RG. No cost.

POC-B: Create one Azure SQL logical server and a single free-tier scratch database (not yet TenantCRM_Test). Confirm the free offer actually applies to this subscription before any further investment, since the free database offer is limited per subscription and eligibility has changed over time.

POC-C: Implement the Phase 0 connection changes (env-driven target, SQL-auth branch). Point the local app at the Azure SQL scratch database using a copy of schema and sample data. Validate core workflows locally against the cloud database. No container, no deployment yet.

POC-D: Write a Dockerfile that installs the Microsoft ODBC Driver for SQL Server and resolves the Reflex frontend (port 3000) / backend (port 8000) split into one deployable image. Build and push to a free registry (GitHub Container Registry or Docker Hub), not ACR. Deploy to the temporary *.azurecontainerapps.io hostname only. Validate scale-to-zero and cold-start behavior.

POC-E: Create one Blob Storage container and validate that a single lease PDF round-trips (upload, restart the container, download) successfully.

POC complete when: the SQL free tier is confirmed at effectively $0, the app runs correctly against Azure SQL, the app runs in a container on Azure without a paid registry, and one file survives a container restart in Blob Storage. Only after this should the full production phasing earlier in this document (custom domain, authentication, Portfolio Manager migration, cross-database refactor) be picked back up.

